{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EuD1m3D249o3PrMBbjgQx8","type":"tool_result","content":"0 {\"claim\": \"Held-out crowd table has 135,630 rows, 52 models, cap 2,713; NBA table has 12,900 rows, 30 teams, cap 258.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n1 {\"claim\": \"Held-out crowd table built from 135,634 raw rows after removing 2 content-overlap rows and 2 rare-participant rows (652 prompt-only overlaps kept); source: derived/task_meta/final_meta.json (copy, name lists removed).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n2 {\"claim\": \"Development tables: Arena 55k 57,477 rows, 64 models (cap 1,150 derived); MT-Bench human 3,355 rows, 6 models (cap 68 derived); sizes from derived/task_meta/search_meta.json (copy, name lists removed).\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.search_score\"]}\n3 {\"claim\": \"Verified removal budgets of the studied configuration: crowd top 62, crowd mid 4 rows; NBA top 62, NBA mid 8 rows; all four flipped within the cap.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.flip_rate\"]}\n4 {\"claim\": \"Budgets as percent of all comparisons (derived k/N): 0.046%, 0.003%, 0.48%, 0.062%.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n5 {\"claim\": \"Both held-out episodes (seeds 21 and 22) returned identical per-case budgets; recorded durations 4.45 s and 4.51 s.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n6 {\"claim\": \"Held-out family scores 0.988 (crowd) and 0.864 (sports); overall 0.926; mean k/cap 0.074; flip rate 1.0.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.score_crowd\", \"final.metric.score_sports\", \"final.metric.mean_budget_fraction\", \"final.metric.flip_rate\"]}\n7 {\"claim\": \"Random-removal reference score 0.041 and influence-only reference score 0.926; the latter equals the held-out overall score.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.reference_random_score\", \"final.metric.reference_influence_score\", \"final.metric.combined_score\"]}\n8 {\"claim\": \"Random removal flipped 0/20 draws for F1, F3, F4 and 6/20 for F2 (and 7/20 for control F5, 0/20 for F6).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n9 {\"claim\": \"Influence-only (B2) budgets equal the studied configuration's in all four real cases and both permutation controls: 62, 4, 62, 8, and 2, 10.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.reference_influence_score\"]}\n10 {\"claim\": \"Opponent-targeted (B3) budgets: 650, 6, 88, 12 rows for F1 to F4 (ratios to studied 10.5, 1.5, 1.4, 1.5).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n11 {\"claim\": \"Top-pair share ratio sports/crowd is 10.5 and mid-pair ratio is 21.0; the top-pair ratio equals the table-size ratio 135,630/12,900.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n12 {\"claim\": \"Mean comparisons per participant (2N/M) are 5,217 (crowd) and 860 (sports); 62 rows are 1.2% and 7.2% of that (about 6x); mid budgets 0.077% and 0.93% (about 12x); sports has the larger share under both normalisers (10.5x and 21.0x under k/N).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n13 {\"claim\": \"Sports top-pair budget uses 24% of its 258-row cap.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n14 {\"claim\": \"Empty-set control flips no case; cross-table copy control flips 0 of 2 trials.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n15 {\"claim\": \"Permutation controls: moved fraction 0.660 vs expected 0.662 (crowd), 0.498 vs 0.500 (sports); rating-spread ratios 0.088 and 0.160; non-vacuous.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n16 {\"claim\": \"Permuted top pairs flipped with 2 rows (crowd) and 10 rows (sports) versus 62 on real tables; control gap +0.112.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.control_gap_permuted_minus_real\"]}\n17 {\"claim\": \"Held-out evaluation status completed with outcome qualified and success indicator true; qualification gate criteria are not recorded in the evidence, so qualified is not read as certifying controls or validity; candidate diagnostics unverified and unused.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.correct\", \"study.final.outcome\", \"\n18 {\"claim\": \"Investigation found no winner: three search configurations were scored (3 of 3, 0 unscored), each 0.4993 equal to the starting baseline 0.4993, difference 0.0; selection kind non-winner candidate.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.search_score\", \"study.baseline_score\", \"study.search_score_delta\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"study.attempts.unscored\", \"study.investigation_outc\n19 {\"claim\": \"Search-stage evaluator output (quoted from log; no registry entry beyond the 0.4993 total) for the studied configuration: crowd 0.9987, expert 0.0, flip rate 0.5, random reference 0.0695, influence reference 0.4993, mean budget fraction 0.5007, control gap 0.3153; total scores of all three configurations 0.4993 with runtimes about 1.3 s, 2.0 s, 114 s; configurations 2 and 3 identified b\n20 {\"claim\": \"Neither expert case flipped within the 68-row cap while both crowd cases flipped; the crowd cases together needed three rows on average (derived from crowd score 0.9987 and cap 1,150); cause of expert non-flip unknown.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\", \"study.search_score\"]}\n21 {\"claim\": \"The starting baseline was not run on held-out tables; selected configuration equals the baseline's development score.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.baseline_score\", \"study.search_score_delta\"]}\n22 {\"claim\": \"Bradley-Terry model extensions and estimation literature described at title level (ties, order effects, multivariate, Bayesian, existence of MLE, ridge, computation).\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.s2.3833525afb515fbf56c616b5ae5470247fc38fa5\", \"ref.s2.14b981837cc0ff653984dc44f5ae40d75a2dc938\", \"ref.s2.67d7d2cbe825822f53d16390b2fba0cd26e4a77d\", \"ref.s2.41cd421312e118caeae3f9259300550be21ea91d\", \"\n23 {\"claim\": \"Sampling-based uncertainty for BT scores and ranks, and growing-parameter asymptotics, are studied in cited work (title-level description).\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.s2.c8b7881fa0b8f19d275ef66fd79deccd3ccac133\", \"ref.s2.b65b46292745d2301a8464eb8c50fe716086fd1e\", \"ref.s2.7c12004f442c5d7a8baa1e2fc3e38d0459077059\", \"ref.s2.930c1f02b32baa6e47106b7549a156f2eaebc8af\", \"ref.s2.ae7dcf7d85c044ea973d\n24 {\"claim\": \"Broderick et al. propose a finite-sample robustness metric for when dropping a little data changes conclusions.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.s2.f4c5a3ee3ca4435f558b2ec248eb0afb501d8f12\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Broderick2020AnAF\"]}\n25 {\"claim\": \"Chatbot Arena is a crowd-vote platform that aggregates pairwise preferences into rankings.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4392616695\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132\"]}\n26 {\"claim\": \"Influence-function analysis of removing a training point motivates the Newton-step row ordering.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2597603852\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730\"]}\n27 {\"claim\": \"Prior work studies vote-rigging strategies against Chatbot Arena and their effect on model rankings; our deletion-only setting is not compared numerically.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W4406975857\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858\"]}\n28 {\"claim\": \"Evaluator source for comparators B1-B3 and the controls is not staged; only recorded outcomes (flip counts, budgets, moved fractions, spread ratios, copy flips) are reported and construction details are not claimed.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13d94285-5611-4acf-b46b-45a0ef63f49b","uuid":"6f8fc7b1-7da2-4199-bba3-dbc60f0ce753","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:34:23.551Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"0 {\"claim\": \"Held